 `--pull` is strictly read-only (dry-run pass: no rerun, no rebase, no merge, no thread resolution). It returns one bounded, actionable-only payload — `blockers[]`, classified `requiredChecks`, failed-check log `failures[]` (matrix-deduped), and every unresolved `reviewThreads[]` — so you get "everything blocking this PR + what to fix" in one call. Passing checks and satisfied policy are omitted.
 
+## Contradiction incidents and control-plane bootstrap
+
+Retain the complete `--bundle --json` output and runtime/provider versions before diagnosing a verdict contradiction. One immediate `--pull` reconciliation is enough. If the same exact remote head still produces an impossible or internally inconsistent verdict, treat it as a release-control-plane incident; do not normalize repeated polling. A contradiction that persists for 15 minutes is explicitly escalated with the retained operands and both installed/candidate results.
+
+Candidate Shepherd code must never authorize its own product merge. When the installed merge authority is defective, pause the product train and create an isolated control-plane break-fix PR from the stable base. Independently review, merge, verify, and activate that runtime before resuming the product lane. Comparing `forge shepherd` with a worktree CLI is useful shadow evidence, not permission to bypass the installed authority.
+
+Shepherd still **never merges**. A separate durable transition controller may consume `events --since` and lease idempotent merge → exact-merge verify → evidence → closure → next-at-bat actions, but it must recheck the exact head, ownership, reviews, threads, checks, settle, and issue state before each mutation. Until that controller is implemented and enabled, `MERGE_READY` remains a handoff, not an action.
